Since ZX calculus was invented to represent quantum circuits, most of its literature differentiates between input and output legs. 
Following the conventional bra-ket notaion where $\ket{a}$ denotes a state (column vector) and $\bra{a}$ denotes its dual state (row vector, also called as effect), $z,x$ and $H$ nodes can be represented as follows:
\begin{align*}
	z_{\phi} &= \ket{0}^{\otimes n}\bra{0}^{\otimes m} + e^{i \phi} \ket{1}^{\otimes n}\bra{1}^{\otimes m} \\
	H &=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(\ket{0}\bra{0} + \ket{0}\bra{1} + \ket{1}\bra{0} - \ket{1}\bra{1}) \\
	x_{\phi} &=  \ket{+}^{\otimes n}\bra{+}^{\otimes m} + e^{i \phi} \ket{-}^{\otimes n}\bra{-}^{\otimes m}
\end{align*}
where $\ket{+}$ and $\ket{-}$ denotes the orthonormal basis $\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(\ket{0} + \ket{1})$ and $\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(\ket{0} - \ket{1})$ respectively.
Note that $H\ket{+} = \ket{0}$, $H\ket{-} = \ket{1}$, and $HH = I$.

The second part of ZX calculus is its rewrite rules,
which are the statements that certain combinations of generators are equivalent to each other.
Two ZX diagrams are equivalent if they represent the same tensor or they represent the same tensor upto a global constant. (This is because in quantum computation a global constant does not change the result of a quantum circuit.)
Throughout this disseration, a strict equal will be denoted by a $=$ sign, while an equality upto a global constant will be denoted by a $\propto$ sign. \footnote{In the literature of ZX calculus, often the equal $=$ sign denotes both strict equality and equality upto a global constant.}
There are infinitely many rewrite rules, but the followings are the most crucial ones.
\begin{definition}[Rewrite rules of ZX diagrams]
	Eight of the most important rewrite rules are shown in figure \ref{fig:zx_rewrites} and explained as follows:
	Most of these rules are 
\begin{enumerate}
	\item Spider fusion, denoted as $\color{red} \mathbf{f}$. 
		A ZX diagram only consists of $z$-spiders is equivalent to a single $z$-spider with the sum of the phases of the original spiders as its phase.
	\item color change, denoted as $\color{red} \mathbf{c}$.
		A $z$-spider with phase $\phi$ is equivalent to a $x$-spider with phase $\phi$ conjugated by Hadamard gates on all its legs.
	\item Hadamard cancellation, denoted as $\color{red} \mathbf{hh}$.
	\item Identity removal, denoted as $\color{red} \mathbf{id}$.
		A single ranked-2 $z$-spider with phase $0$ and one input and one output leg is equivalent to identity ( single bond).
		Two Hadamard gates is equivalent to identity.
	\item $\pi$-copy, denoted as $\color{red} \mathbf{p}$. A $\pi$-phase $x$-spider can be copied through a $z$-spider, and vice versa.
	\item Copy rule, denoted as $\color{red} \mathbf{cp}$. A $0$-phase $x$-spider can be copied through a $z$-spider.
	\item Strong complementarity, denoted as $\color{red} \mathbf{sc}$. 
		Two sets of 0-phased $z$ and $x$ spiders that form a complete bipartite graph can be tranformed into a simpler graph where the set of $z$ spiders are replaced with the same raned $x$-spider, and the set of $x$-spiders be replaced with the same ranked $z$-spider.
		A This is the most \emph{crucial} one for our later discussion. 
	\item Euler decomposition, denoted as $\color{red} \mathbf{e}$. 
		Hadamard gate can be decomposed into a sequence of $z$ and $x$ spiders with specific phases following the Euler decomposition of single qubit unitaries.
\end{enumerate}

